The compile error came from `String.matches`, which doesn't exist in Apex. The fix replaces it with the static `Pattern.matches(CODE_PATTERN, normalised)`, which requires the entire trimmed, upper-cased code to match `[A-Z]{3}`. The rest of the class is unchanged.

The deploy failed because each exception class declared a constructor with a `(String)` signature. Apex exception classes already inherit `Exception(String)`, so the explicit constructors clash with it. The fix is to remove those constructors and let the subclasses inherit the built-in ones. `new UnsupportedTermsException('msg')` still compiles, and `catch (PaymentTermsException e)` still catches both subtypes.

