The failure happened because Apex tests cap chained Queueable depth at 5. A single nested chain of 250 jobs for 50,000 Ids cannot run inside one `Test.startTest()`/`Test.stopTest()` block, and the requirement excludes a test-only bypass. The fix is to split the remaining Ids across a small number of child jobs instead of one long chain. Each execution still handles at most 200 accounts with one SOQL query and one DML statement, and it still enqueues jobs for the remaining Ids. Nesting depth for 50,000 Ids is 3, and each job enqueues at most 49 children, which stays under the 50-enqueue-per-transaction limit. This is a deliberate departure from a strict single-successor chain, and I've noted it at the end.

File: force-app/main/default/classes/AccountScoringJob.cls

```apex
public with sharing class AccountScoringJob implements Queueable {

    private static final Integer CHUNK_SIZE = 200;
    // Max child jobs enqueued per execution. Kept below the 50 enqueueJob
    // limit per transaction and keeps nesting shallow (depth 3 for 50,000 Ids).
    private static final Integer MAX_FANOUT = 49;
    private static final Decimal HOT_THRESHOLD = 1000000;
    private static final Decimal WARM_THRESHOLD = 100000;

    private List<Id> accountIds;

    public AccountScoringJob(List<Id> accountIds) {
        this.accountIds = (accountIds == null) ? new List<Id>() : accountIds;
    }

    /**
     * Enqueues the first job for the given Ids and returns its job Id.
     * A null or empty list enqueues nothing and returns null.
     */
    public static Id start(List<Id> accountIds) {
        if (accountIds == null || accountIds.isEmpty()) {
            return null;
        }
        return System.enqueueJob(new AccountScoringJob(accountIds));
    }

    public void execute(QueueableContext context) {
        if (accountIds == null || accountIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        Integer batchSize = Math.min(CHUNK_SIZE, accountIds.size());

        List<Id> batchIds = new List<Id>();
        for (Integer i = 0; i < batchSize; i++) {
            batchIds.add(accountIds[i]);
        }

        List<Id> remainingIds = new List<Id>();
        for (Integer i = batchSize; i < accountIds.size(); i++) {
            remainingIds.add(accountIds[i]);
        }

        // One SOQL query per execution.
        List<Account> accounts = [
            SELECT Id, AnnualRevenue, Rating
            FROM Account
            WHERE Id IN :batchIds
        ];

        for (Account acc : accounts) {
            acc.Rating = resolveRating(acc.AnnualRevenue);
        }

        // One DML statement per execution.
        if (!accounts.isEmpty()) {
            update accounts;
        }

        // Hand the remaining Ids to child jobs, split into contiguous groups.
        enqueueRemaining(remainingIds);
    }

    private static void enqueueRemaining(List<Id> remainingIds) {
        if (remainingIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        Integer fanout = Math.min(MAX_FANOUT, remainingIds.size());
        Integer groupSize = (remainingIds.size() + fanout - 1) / fanout; // ceiling division

        Integer start = 0;
        while (start < remainingIds.size()) {
            Integer end = Math.min(start + groupSize, remainingIds.size());
            List<Id> group = new List<Id>();
            for (Integer i = start; i < end; i++) {
                group.add(remainingIds[i]);
            }
            System.enqueueJob(new AccountScoringJob(group));
            start = end;
        }
    }

    private static String resolveRating(Decimal annualRevenue) {
        if (annualRevenue == null) {
            return 'Cold';
        }
        if (annualRevenue >= HOT_THRESHOLD) {
            return 'Hot';
        }
        if (annualRevenue >= WARM_THRESHOLD) {
            return 'Warm';
        }
        return 'Cold';
    }
}
```

Deviation from the spec: the spec asks for a single successor job that carries the remaining Ids. A single-successor chain for 50,000 Ids is 250 levels deep, and Apex tests reject anything beyond depth 5, so that design cannot pass the whole-chain test. Each execution now enqueues up to 49 child jobs that partition the remaining Ids. Every account is still rated, each execution still processes at most 200 accounts with one SOQL query and one DML statement, and the test runs within the depth limit. If the requirement to form a single linear chain is strict, the test cannot verify 50,000 accounts in one block.

The error came from nesting `PurgeBatch` inside `LogPurgeJob`, since Apex only allows top-level classes to implement `Database.Batchable`. I've moved the batch logic into `LogPurgeJob` itself, so the class implements both `Schedulable` and `Database.Batchable<SObject>` at the top level. The file list is unchanged, and the public API (no-argument constructor, `execute(SchedulableContext)`, and `scheduleNightly()`) is preserved.

